The element $\gamma$ in <a href="#prop:tame-primes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:tame-primes">7</a> can be chosen so that, in the bases of <a href="#eq:base-green-bases" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:base-green-bases">[eq:base-green-bases]</a>, the obstruction matrix is $$\label{eq:obstruction-matrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}0&\kappa\\-\kappa&0\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad \kappa\in\mathbb Q_p^\times.$$ Thus no nonzero base Greenberg class lifts to order two.

outputs.exact_statement:
The expression $H_\gamma$ is independent of the primitive and bilinear. If a nonzero class $x\in H^1_{\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}}(K,V\otimes k)$ is the specialization of an admissible class in $H^1_{\mathrm{Gr}}(K,M_2^{\eta})$, then $$\label{eq:obstruction-vanishing}
 H_\gamma(x,d)=0
 \quad\text{for every }d\in H^1_{\overline{\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}}}(K,V\otimes k).$$ The same necessary condition holds for either $\eta$.

The element $\gamma$ in <a href="#prop:tame-primes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:tame-primes">7</a> can be chosen so that, in the bases of <a href="#eq:base-green-bases" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:base-green-bases">[eq:base-green-bases]</a>, the obstruction matrix is $$\label{eq:obstruction-matrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}0&\kappa\\-\kappa&0\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad \kappa\in\mathbb Q_p^\times.$$ Thus no nonzero base Greenberg class lifts to order two.

For the primes of <a href="#prop:tame-primes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:tame-primes">7</a>, chosen in the strict case as in <a href="#lem:nonzero-obstruction" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:nonzero-obstruction">11</a>, every fixed $b\ge1$, and either $\eta=1$ or $\eta=-1$, one has $$\label{eq:green-length}
 \mathop{\mathrm{length}}_{R_b}H^1_{\mathrm{Gr}}(L,V\otimes\psi^{\eta})
 \begin{cases}
 =0,&\text{outside the strict case},\\
 \le2,&\text{in the strict case}.
 \end{cases}$$ In the strict case this module is killed by $t$ and is the image of its two-dimensional base space under multiplication by $t^{b-1}$. Put $$\label{eq:b-star}
 b_*=1\quad\text{outside the strict case},\qquad
 b_*=3\quad\text{in the strict case}.$$ Thus in both cases the length in <a href="#eq:green-length" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:green-length">[eq:green-length]</a> is strictly smaller than $b_*$.

At the distinguished place over $p$, and for either sign, $$\label{eq:heegner-log}
 \sum_{g\in\mathcal G_i}(1+t)^{\pm\ell_i(g)}
       \log_{A_0,p}(y_i^g)
       \longrightarrow0\pmod {t^{b_*}}.$$ More precisely, every coefficient of degree less than $b_*$ has valuation tending to $+\infty$ along $\mathcal U$. The coefficients through any other fixed degree have a common lower valuation bound. These assertions hold in a common $p$-adic algebraic closure, with its fixed extension of the valuation.

At a split spherical place the standard parameter of the theta form, in unitary normalization, is $$\label{eq:theta-eigenparameters}
 (\pi\otimes\eta)\ \oplus\ |\cdot|^{-1/2}
                       \ \oplus\ |\cdot|^{1/2}.$$ Here $\pi\otimes\eta$ means tensoring the standard two-dimensional parameter by the determinant-twist character.

There are group-ring polynomial factors $A_i(m),B_i(m)$ with uniformly bounded coefficients such that $$\label{eq:cleared-period-comparison}
 A_i(m)S_i(m)^2
   =B_i(m)P_{0,i}(m)^2P_{1,i}(m)^2.$$ For each $i$, after taking $m$ sufficiently close to zero on its branch, $A_i(m)$ has nonzero augmentation whose valuation is bounded above by a constant independent of $i$. The required neighborhood of zero may depend on $i$. The identity holds in the full characteristic-zero tame group algebra, including at zeros of the global central $L$-values.

There are finite tests satisfying the $p$- and $q_i$-requirements of Section <a href="#sec:theta" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:theta">4</a> for which the coefficient <a href="#eq:primitive-monomial" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:primitive-monomial">[eq:primitive-monomial]</a>, at index $(e_1,e_1)$, unfolds into the product of a fixed row-one Heisenberg theta function and $$\label{eq:row-two-unfolding}
  \int_{[T]}\varphi(s)\eta^{-1}(s)
       \vartheta_{1,d_1}(sb_1)\vartheta_{2,d_2}(sb_2)\,ds.$$ Here $b_1,b_2\in U(1)(\mathbb A_F)$ translate the binary lines, and known splitting-character factors in $b_j$ have been removed. At $q_i$ the only additional row-two condition is a unit cut in the first binary column. The unfolding introduces a fixed nonzero volume factor and no factor $q_i-1$.

The functor $\mathcal D_d$ is finite locally free and commutes with base change. Integral changes of Hodge frame preserve its lattice. If a map on $\mathcal H$ is diagonal with entries $c_1,c_2,c_3$, its action on the basis dual to the minor monomials has entries $$(c_1c_2)^{a_{12}}(c_1c_3)^{a_{13}}
          (c_2c_3)^{a_{23}},
       \qquad a_{12}+a_{13}+a_{23}=d.$$ A filtration of $\mathcal H$ by subbundles with constant locally free graded pieces induces such a filtration on $\mathcal D_d(\mathcal H)$.
